The '''2006 UEFA Champions League final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] of Spain and [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] of England at the [[Stade de France]] in [[Saint-Denis, Seine-Saint-Denis|Saint-Denis]], [[Paris]], France, on Wednesday, 17 May 2006. It was the final match of the [[2005–06 UEFA Champions League|2005–06 season]] of Europe's primary cup competition, the [[UEFA Champions League]]. Barcelona were appearing in their fifth final, having won the competition once in [[1992 European Cup final|1992]], while it was Arsenal's first final appearance and the first appearance by a club from London.

Each club progressed through the group stage and three knockout rounds to reach the final, playing 12 matches in total. Barcelona won their group and progressed to the final after beating [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] and [[AC Milan|Milan]], respectively. Arsenal won their group to move into the knockout stage, and progressed without conceding a goal against [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] and [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]], respectively.

A crowd of 79,500 watched as [[Jens Lehmann]] was sent off early in the game. Despite this setback, Arsenal took the lead through [[Sol Campbell]] towards the end of the first half. After an hour, [[Henrik Larsson]] came on for Barcelona and was involved in both of their goals. Barcelona equalised through [[Samuel Eto'o]] near the end of the second half, and a few minutes later, [[Juliano Belletti]] scored to give Barcelona a 2–1 victory.

==Background==
It was the first time Arsenal had appeared in the European Cup final, which also made them the first club from [[London]] to do so.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Arsenal aim to upset the odds |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4750383.stm |website=BBC Sport |date=16 May 2006 |access-date=25 November 2008}}</ref> Arsenal's appearance in the match came six years to the day since their last European final, when they faced [[Galatasaray S.K. (football)|Galatasaray]] in the [[2000 UEFA Cup final]]; they lost on penalties after the match had finished goalless after [[extra time]]. Of the players who featured in that final, only [[Thierry Henry]] and [[Dennis Bergkamp]] remained at the club, while [[Sylvinho]], who had featured for Arsenal in the final, was now a Barcelona player.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/ucl/2005/e/e_1108503_pk.pdf |title=Match Press Kit |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=16 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081217232132/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/ucl/2005/e/e_1108503_pk.pdf |archive-date=17 December 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Barcelona were appearing in the final for the fifth time; their last appearance was a 4–0 loss to [[AC Milan|Milan]] in [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]]. Two more losses preceded the 1994 final ([[1961 European Cup final|1961]] and [[1986 European Cup final|1986]]), and their only success in the competition came in [[1992 European Cup final|1992]] when they beat [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.fcbarcelona.cat/web/english/club/historia/etapes_historia/etapa_4.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20080621071948/http://www.fcbarcelona.cat/web/english/club/historia/etapes_historia/etapa_4.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 June 2008 |title=From the 75th Anniversary to the European Cup (1974–1992) |publisher=[[FC Barcelona]] |access-date=28 July 2008}}</ref> Barcelona went into the final as domestic league champions, having won [[La Liga]] a fortnight earlier.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4970966.stm |title=Barca retain Spanish league title |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=3 May 2006 |access-date=25 November 2008}}</ref> They were regarded as the best side on the continent because they possessed players such as [[Ronaldinho]] and [[Samuel Eto'o]]. Barcelona player [[Deco]] dismissed the notion of Barcelona being favourites, insisting they would not be complacent: "Milan were winning 3–0 last year against Liverpool and ended up losing in the end. We need to be serious, calm and fully concentrated so that we don't make any errors".<ref name="Deco">{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4773247.stm |title=Deco shrugging off favourites tag |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 May 2006 |access-date=25 November 2008}}</ref>

Neither side had been beaten en route to the final, with Arsenal only conceding two goals in their 12 matches before the final, including ten successive matches without conceding. This set the record for the longest time without a conceding a goal, having played 919 minutes since conceding against [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] in the group stage. Barcelona, on the other hand, had scored 114 goals in all competitions before the final. The two finalists were guaranteed a financial windfall from their progress to the final. Arsenal would receive a revenue of around €37.3&nbsp;million if they won and approximately €34.7&nbsp;million if they lost. Barcelona would earn €31.5&nbsp;million from revenue if they won, and around €28.9&nbsp;million if they were to lose. Included in the total for participating in the final, the UEFA Champions League winners would collect approximately €6.4&nbsp;million, with the runners-up receiving approximately €3.8&nbsp;million.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=pr.html |title=Purists set for dream final |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=16 May 2006 |access-date=25 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090108025750/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season%3D2005/round%3D2205/match%3D1108503/report%3Dpr.html |archive-date=8 January 2009 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The final marked the appearance of a [[European Champion Club's Cup|new trophy]] following [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]'s triumph over Milan the [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|previous year]]. As Liverpool won the competition for the fifth time they were entitled to keep the trophy, creating the need for a new one.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4581555.stm |title=Merseyside revels in Reds victory |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 May 2005 |access-date=25 November 2008}}</ref>

==Venue==
UEFA awarded the right to host the final to the Stade de France in [[Saint-Denis, Seine-Saint-Denis|Saint-Denis]] after considering its merits in terms of capacity, stadium infrastructure and dossiers including city and airport agreements and promotional plans. UEFA also conducted a variety of site visits.<ref name="background">{{Cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=mb.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070317233504/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=mb.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=17 March 2007 |title=Match Background |publisher=[[UEFA]] |access-date=9 September 2007}}</ref> The Stade de France had previously staged the [[2000 UEFA Champions League final]] between Real Madrid and [[Valencia CF|Valencia]], which Madrid won 3–0. Saint-Denis was chosen to host the final in recognition of the 50th anniversary of the first European Cup final in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], held in nearby [[Paris]] at the [[Parc des Princes]], now home of [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Anniversary reason for Paris decision |url=http://en.uefa.com/uefa/socialresponsibility/newsid=298133.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120708092612/http://en.uefa.com/uefa/socialresponsibility/newsid=298133.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=8 July 2012 |work=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 April 2005 |access-date=21 June 2010}}</ref> The Parc des Princes also hosted the [[1975 European Cup final|1975]] and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]] European Cup finals, the [[1978 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1978]] and [[1995 UEFA Cup Winners' Cup final|1995]] [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] finals, and the [[1998 UEFA Cup final]].<ref name="background"/>

==Route to the final==
{{further|2005–06 UEFA Champions League}}

Teams qualified for the Champions League group stage, either directly or through three preliminary rounds, based on both their position in the preceding domestic league and [[UEFA coefficient#League coefficient|the strength of that league]]. Both Arsenal and Barcelona entered the competition in the group stages: Arsenal by finishing second in the [[2004–05 FA Premier League]], and Barcelona by winning [[2004–05 La Liga|La Liga]]. The group stages were contested as eight [[round robin tournament|double round robin groups]] of four teams, with the top two qualifying for the knockout stage. Knockout matches were decided based on [[two-legged match|home and away matches]], with the [[away goals rule]], [[extra time]] and [[penalty shootout (football)|penalty shootout]]s as tiebreakers if needed.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Regulations of the UEFA Champions League 2005/06 |url=https://www.uefa.com/newsfiles/19071.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20051020013336/http://www.uefa.com/newsfiles/19071.pdf |year=2005 |archive-date=20 October 2005 |access-date=16 July 2008 |publisher=[[UEFA]]}}</ref>

