==Pre-match==
[[Image:Barcelona vs Arsenal Teams line up.jpg|thumb|right|The two teams line up before kick off]]

===Related events===
Barcelona showed the match live from their [[Mini Estadi]] on a {{convert|70|m|ft|adj=on}} television screen for an estimated audience of 15,276. Before the final, an estimated 1.2&nbsp;million people descended into the streets of Barcelona, as the team toured the city on two open-top double-decker buses to celebrate winning La Liga.

[[File:UEFA Champions League Final 2006 - Team flags.jpg|left|thumb|The team flags hoisted above the pitch before the kick-off]]

===Officials===
On the morning of the final it emerged that the assistant referee for the game [[Ole Hermann Borgan]] had posed for photographs in a Barcelona shirt for ''[[Drammens Tidende]]'', a Norwegian newspaper. After initially sticking by the official, UEFA eventually decided to replace him with fellow Norwegian Arild Sundet. Norway's head of refereeing Rune Pedersen stated: "It is an unwritten rule that referees should not do anything that can doubt their impartial stance".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4986436.stm |title=Final linesman denies Barca bias |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 May 2006 |access-date=25 November 2008}}</ref>

===Team selection===
Barcelona were expected to line up in a [[Formation (association football)#4–2–3–1|4–2–3–1]] formation, with Ronaldinho, [[Ludovic Giuly]] and Deco supporting Samuel Eto'o who would be deployed as the lone [[Forward (association football)|striker]].<ref name="lineup">{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/henrywinter/2336987/Wengers-pledge-gives-fans-cause-for-optimism.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/columnists/henrywinter/2336987/Wengers-pledge-gives-fans-cause-for-optimism.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Wenger's pledge gives fans cause for optimism |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=15 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 |location=London |first=Henry |last=Winter}}{{cbignore}}</ref> They had doubts about the fitness of [[Lionel Messi]] going into the final. He had pulled a thigh muscle during the second leg of their match with Chelsea, and had not played since, though he was included in the 22-man squad for the final. Barcelona coach [[Frank Rijkaard]] stated he would leave it late before deciding whether to select him.<ref name="Deco" /> Despite being included in the squad, Messi did not feature in the final. Arsenal were expected to line up in a [[Formation (association football)#4–5–1|4–5–1]] formation, with [[Thierry Henry]] as the sole striker. There was much discussion about whether [[José Antonio Reyes]] would take the place of [[Robert Pires]] on the left of midfield.<ref name="lineup" /> It had been announced before the match that this would be Pires' last match as he had agreed to join [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] next season.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsenal-1-barcelona-2-barcelona-crush-heroic-arsenal-in-space-of-four-brutal-minutes-478659.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/arsenal-1-barcelona-2-barcelona-crush-heroic-arsenal-in-space-of-four-brutal-minutes-478659.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Arsenal 1 Barcelona 2: Barcelona crush heroic Arsenal in space of four brutal minutes |work=[[The Independent]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=27 November 2008 |location=London |first=Sam |last=Wallace}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

Ultimately, Barcelona fielded a [[Formation (association football)#4–3–3|4–3–3]] formation with [[Mark van Bommel]] and [[Edmílson]] lining up in midfield alongside Deco, ahead of [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]] and [[Andrés Iniesta]]. Lionel Messi did not feature in the matchday squad, despite returning from injury and featuring in the 22-man squad, while [[Henrik Larsson]] was among the substitutes in potentially his last game for the club. Arsenal fielded a  [[Formation (association football)#4–4–1–1|4–4–1–1]] formation, which saw [[Emmanuel Eboué]] replace the injured [[Lauren (Cameroonian footballer)|Lauren]], and [[Ashley Cole]] return at left-back to make only his third appearance in the competition that season due to injury. Thierry Henry was deployed as the lone striker, with [[Freddie Ljungberg]] playing off his shoulder.<ref name="minute" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Tactical line-up |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=tl.html |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=17 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090108001228/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season%3D2005/round%3D2205/match%3D1108503/report%3Dtl.html |archive-date=8 January 2009 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
====First half====
[[File:UEFA Champions League Final 2006 - Playing.jpg|thumb|right|Play during the first half]]
As both teams' first-choice kits featured a shade of red, Arsenal wore their yellow away strip, while Barcelona wore their traditional blue and maroon striped kit. Arsenal won the [[Coin flipping|coin toss]] and Barcelona kicked off.<ref name="guardian">{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/minbymin/report/0,,1777091,00.html |title=Arsenal v Barcelona Live |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=17 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 |location=London |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081017130217/http://football.guardian.co.uk/minbymin/report/0%2C%2C1777091%2C00.html |archive-date=17 October 2008 |url-status=live}}</ref> Barcelona almost immediately came under pressure when Thierry Henry shot straight at Barcelona goalkeeper [[Víctor Valdés]], who conceded a [[Corner kick|corner]]. From the resulting corner Arsenal had another chance again courtesy of Henry, whose shot was again saved by Valdés. The next attack in the seventh minute resulted in Arsenal goalkeeper [[Jens Lehmann]] saving from Ludovic Giuly after he shot from a narrow angle. Four minutes later Barcelona were awarded a [[Direct free kick|free-kick]] 35 yards from goal; Ronaldinho shot wide of the goal.

With 18 minutes played, Lehmann became the first player to be [[Misconduct (football)|sent off]] in a European Cup final after he brought down [[Samuel Eto'o]] outside the Arsenal box.<ref name="bbc">{{cite news |title=Referee regrets Lehmann red card |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4993994.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=12 February 2011}}</ref> Giuly subsequently put the ball in the back of the net, but the goal was not allowed due to the foul. Arsenal substituted Robert Pires for [[Manuel Almunia]], their substitute goalkeeper.<ref name="minute">{{Cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=mbm.html |title=Minute by Minute |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=17 May 2006 |access-date=9 September 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070317233533/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=mbm.html|archive-date=17 March 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref> The free-kick was sent wide by Ronaldinho. Following Lehmann's dismissal Barcelona exerted more pressure on Arsenal, to the point where Emmanuel Eboué was cautioned for a high [[Tackle (football move)#Association football|tackle]].<ref name="minute" />

In the 37th minute Arsenal overcame their numerical disadvantage to score when [[Sol Campbell]] headed in from a Thierry Henry free-kick following [[Carles Puyol]]'s foul on Emmanuel Eboué. Some believed that Eboué was guilty of [[Diving (association football)|diving]].<ref name="guardian" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Wenger Slams Eboue For Diving |publisher=MTN Football |date=21 May 2006 |access-date=11 January 2009 |url=http://www.mtnfootball.com/live/content.php?Item_ID=3496&Revision=en%2F1 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110816021817/http://www.mtnfootball.com/live/content.php?Item_ID=3496&Revision=en%2F1 |archive-date=16 August 2011}}</ref><ref name="BBC2006">{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4773353.stm |title=Barcelona 2–1 Arsenal |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=17 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071009001809/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4773353.stm| archive-date=9 October 2007| url-status= live}}</ref> Arsenal held onto their lead for the remainder of the half. Barcelona's best chance came from Eto'o, whose shot hit the post via a save from Almunia.<ref name="BBC2006"/>

====Second half====
At half-time, Barcelona replaced Edmílson, who had picked up an injury in the first half, with Andrés Iniesta.<ref name="minute" /> Almunia saved a shot from Iniesta six minutes into the half.

After 61 minutes, Barcelona substituted Van Bommel for Larsson.<ref name="minute" /> Minutes later, [[Alexander Hleb]] received a pass from Henry but shot wide of the goal. Arsenal began to press forward and both Henry and Ljungberg saw shots saved; Henry then had another chance to score on the counter after being put through by Hleb, but his scuffed shot was straight at Valdés.<ref name="guardian" /> Following this attack, [[Oleguer Presas|Oleguer]] was replaced by [[Juliano Belletti]], and Arsenal replaced [[Cesc Fàbregas]] with [[Mathieu Flamini]].<ref name="guardian" />

Next, Iniesta sent a pass through the inside-left channel to Larsson whose one-touch, right-footed lay-off quickly released Eto'o to equalise for Barcelona.<ref name="minute" /> Four minutes later, Larsson was again the provider, playing a one-two with Belletti in the inside-right channel, before the Brazilian full-back shot right-footed through Almunia's legs to score at the near post.<ref name="guardian"/>

The remaining minutes saw Arsenal replace Hleb with Reyes in an attempt to equalise, but Barcelona had the best chance during this period, only for Giuly to hit his shot straight at Almunia. Following this, Larsson impeded the Arsenal goalkeeper and was cautioned.<ref name="minute" /> The match finished soon after with Barcelona winning 2–1. With this defeat, Arsenal became the first English club and third overall – after [[Hamburger SV]] and [[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]] – to have been runner-up in [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the three main European club competitions|all three major European competitions]] ([[UEFA Champions League|European Champion Clubs' Cup/UEFA Champions League]], UEFA Cup/UEFA Europa League, and the now-defunct [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]]).<ref>{{cite web |author=Roberto Di Maggio |url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/intfinalrec.html |title=International Finalists |work=Record Sport Soccer Statistics Foundation |date=18 February 2021}}</ref>

