==Post-match==
[[Image:Barca medals.jpg|thumb|right|The Barcelona players receiving their medals]]
Barcelona captain Carles Puyol collected the trophy from UEFA president [[Lennart Johansson]] as Barcelona celebrated their second European Cup triumph.<ref name="guardian" />

Much of the discussion after the match centred around referee [[Terje Hauge]]'s decision to send off Lehmann. Football pundit [[Mark Lawrenson]] stated: "The game changed when Arsenal goalkeeper Jens Lehmann was sent off".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4992230.stm |title=Experts views on Arsenal |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=28 November 2008}}</ref> Arsenal manager Arsène Wenger agreed: "When Jens Lehmann got sent off, that left us with 70 minutes to play with ten against eleven, against a team that retains the ball very well".<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2005/round=2205/match=1108503/report=rw.html |title=Valdés save vital to victory |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=17 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090108130502/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season%3D2005/round%3D2205/match%3D1108503/report%3Drw.html |archive-date=8 January 2009 |url-status=dead}}</ref> He refused to openly criticise this decision, although he did criticise the decision to award Barcelona's first goal, as he felt that Samuel Eto'o was [[Offside (association football)|offside]]. Wenger was also adamant that his young side would bounce back from the defeat.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4771959.stm |title=Wenger left frustrated by defeat |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=26 November 2008}}</ref> One person who did criticise the referee was Thierry Henry, stating: "I was kicked all over the place. I expected the referee to do his job. I don't think he did".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/4992172.stm |title=Furious Henry hits out at referee |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=27 November 2008}}</ref> There was much discussion after the match over whether Henry would remain with Arsenal or move to Barcelona, whom he had been linked with a move to over the past months. Arsène Wenger was asked post match if Henry would leave, to which the Arsenal manager replied: "I don't know. I don't think so".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/4992674.stm |title=Wenger hopeful of Henry staying |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=27 November 2008}}</ref> However, on 19 May, Henry signed a new four-year contract with Arsenal, ending the speculation about a possible move to Barcelona.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/4996270.stm |title=Henry to stay a Gunner until 2010 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 May 2005 |access-date=27 November 2008}}</ref>

[[Henrik Larsson]], who provided the two assists for the goals, was delighted with the triumph and had no regrets over deciding to leave Barcelona after the match: "I want to play football. I feel I haven't played as much as I would love to because of the great players we have at Barcelona". Fellow Barcelona player [[Giovanni van Bronckhorst]] was emotional as he won against his former club: "It's special to win the final, and even more special to do it against your old team". Samuel Eto'o, scorer of Barcelona's first goal, cited Liverpool's comeback against Milan as inspiration for Barcelona's victory: "After having seen what Liverpool did last year, we told ourselves not to throw in the towel. We followed Liverpool's example". Barcelona manager Frank Rijkaard praised the role of goalkeeper Victor Valdés in their victory, stating that he made a number of important saves to keep them in the match: "Valdes played a decisive role. He saved us at crucial moments".<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/4772009.stm |title=Larsson excited about his future |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=18 May 2006 |access-date=27 November 2008}}</ref> Talking to ''[[FourFourTwo]]'' in 2017, [[Deco]] regarded Henry missing a chance to make it 2–0 to Arsenal when through one-on-one with Valdés with just over 20 minutes remaining as being a decisive moment in the game.<ref>{{cite news |last=Brewin |first=Joe |title=Deco: We wouldn't have beaten Arsenal in 2006 if Valdes hadn't saved us |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/deco-we-wouldnt-have-beaten-arsenal-2006-if-valdes-hadnt-saved-us |date=22 May 2017|access-date=2 June 2026 |work=FourFourTwo}}</ref>

Thierry Henry paid tribute to Larsson's contribution to Barcelona's win after the game, saying, "People always talk about Ronaldinho, Eto'o, Giuly and everything, but I didn't see them today, I saw Henrik Larsson. He came on, he changed the game, that is what killed the game. Sometimes you talk about Ronaldinho and Eto'o and people like that; you need to talk about the proper footballer who made the difference, and that was Henrik Larsson tonight."<ref name=Henry/> Henry also remarked, "You have to remember that Barcelona is a team. It's not Eto'o, it's not Ronaldinho. On Wednesday, the man who made the difference was Henrik Larsson. You have to give him credit for that because when he came on he made some amazing runs around the box and he showed he is a team player. You saw two times he fed the ball back to people and that's what I always talk about, he is a team player. He did that against us, even though the first goal was a close offside decision."<ref name=amazing/> Larsson's introduction giving Barcelona the cutting edge required to overcome Arsenal was noted by international press.<ref name=Henry>{{cite web |title=Henry questions referee |url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,11670_2377139,00.html |publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |access-date=19 September 2020}}</ref><ref name=amazing>{{Cite web |url=http://www.arsenal.com/news/news-archive/henry-amazing-larsson-made-the-difference |title="Amazing Larsson made the difference" Arsenal.com |access-date=23 July 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160809132317/http://www.arsenal.com/news/news-archive/henry-amazing-larsson-made-the-difference |archive-date=9 August 2016 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all}}</ref><ref name=Brodkin>{{cite web |title=Larsson takes his leave in the grandest style |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/may/18/championsleague200506.championsleague1 |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=19 September 2020}}</ref><ref name=bleacher>{{cite web |title=5 Star Names of Champions League Finals for Barcelona |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2479846-5-star-names-of-champions-league-finals-for-barcelona |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |access-date=19 September 2020}}</ref>

As a result of winning the Champions League, Barcelona would face [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]], the winners of the [[UEFA Cup]], in the [[UEFA Super Cup]]. Played on 25 August 2006, the [[2006 UEFA Super Cup|match was won 3–0]] by Sevilla.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/5287976.stm |title=Barcelona 0–3 Sevilla |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 August 2006 |access-date=10 August 2008}}</ref> Barcelona's victory meant they would also participate in the [[FIFA Club World Cup]]. Receiving a bye in the first round, they beat Mexicans [[Club América]] 4–0 in the semi-finals to progress to [[2006 FIFA Club World Cup final|the final]] where they faced [[Copa Libertadores]] champions [[Sport Club Internacional|Internacional]] of Brazil, who won 1–0.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/6187783.stm |title=Internacional sink Barca in final |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=17 December 2006 |access-date=30 November 2008 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090101130101/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/6187783.stm| archive-date= 1 January 2009 | url-status= live}}</ref>

